# local-jax-cpu-2b — authoritative configuration.
#
# The directory name is documentation; this file decides. Never copy a slot value
# out of the directory name into a CLI flag (NAMING.md, "The name is a claim").
#
# Committed on purpose and deliberately NOT gitignored. A real environment
# variable always wins over it: mcp-run.sh exports only unset keys, Makefiles use
# ?=, and dotenv does not overwrite.
#
# The file is still called tpu.env, on every rig in this monorepo including the
# ones with no TPU. It is the name the loaders, the Makefiles and the tests all
# agree on, and renaming it here would buy accuracy in one filename at the cost
# of the one thing that makes a monorepo of near-identical rigs navigable.
#
# STATUS as of 2026-08-29: forked from `gpu-jax-g4dn-2b` and retargeted from an
# EC2 GPU to the local CPU. Values marked MEASURED HERE were measured on this
# host. Values marked INHERITED were measured on Turing GPUs by
# `gpu-jax-g5g-2b`; they are kept where they describe the CHECKPOINT or the
# ENGINE, which are the same objects here, and deleted where they describe a
# chip. Nothing in this file is a throughput result for this rig.
#
# WHY THIS RIG EXISTS: it is the zero point. Every other rig here measures an
# accelerator and none of them can separate the chip from the engine, because
# there is no run without a chip. This is the only place a number for this port
# can be attributed entirely to the model code and the XLA CPU backend — and the
# only rig with no hourly cost and no capacity risk, which makes it the right
# place to reproduce an engine-level bug before spending a capacity cycle on one.

# --- Model -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reference instruction-tuned release, so the rig name carries no encoding
# slot. MODELS.md: 10.2 GB on disk, 9.26 GB of bf16 text-decoder weights once the
# non-text towers are dropped, 18 KiB/token of KV.
#
# Readable ANONYMOUSLY — VERIFIED HERE 2026-08-29 by listing and fetching from
# the Hub with token=False. No Hugging Face token is needed for this checkpoint,
# which is why there is no Secrets Manager equivalent in this rig. save_hf_token
# exists for a gated checkpoint and writes ~/.cache/huggingface/token at 0600.
#
# Deliberately NOT the -qat-w4a16-ct export the TPU JAX rig serves, and the
# reason is DIFFERENT from the GPU siblings' reason. There the fused W4A16 Pallas
# kernel cannot run because Pallas lowers through Triton and Turing caps shared
# memory at 64 KiB per block. Here Pallas has no backend at all, so
# JAX_E_PALLAS_INTERPRET auto-enables and the kernel runs in INTERPRET MODE — a
# simulator. It would produce correct numbers at a speed that means nothing, and
# `pallas_interpret=True` in the startup banner is the one line that tells you so.
# Read that banner before believing any w4a16 number from this rig.
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it

# auto reads the mode off the checkpoint name (w4a16 if the name says so, else
# fp16). Pinned to fp16 here because the checkpoint above is dense.
QUANT_MODE=fp16

# --- Hardware ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Slot 3 is `cpu`, which NAMING.md already specifies as the hardware value for a
# CPU-only rig. Slot 1 is `local`, added to NAMING.md 2026-08-29: this is the one
# execution target with no control plane at all — nothing is provisioned, nothing
# is billed, and there is no API that can refuse.
#
# MEASURED HERE 2026-08-29 on the development host:
#   CPU        13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-1360P, 16 logical cores
#   RAM        14.3 GiB total (MemTotal 14,682,148 kB)
#   Swap       none originally; 16 GiB added — see the swap note below
#   Root fs    btrfs on /dev/vdb, 154 GB free
#
# THE HOST IS NOT PART OF THE RIG. Unlike every sibling, whose hardware slot
# names a specific SKU the rig provisions, `cpu` names whatever CPU the rig is
# checked out on. Two runs of this rig on two machines are not comparable, and a
# benchmark report from here MUST record the host in its own fields — the rig
# name cannot carry it.
ACCELERATOR_TYPE=none
CHIP_COUNT=0

# --- Precision ---------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEVICE DECIDES, not this file. ports/gemma4/jax_e_model.py reads the live
# device and picks; DTYPE is the override and JAX_E_COMPUTE_DTYPE is the escape
# hatch. On CPU there is no compute capability to read, so IS_PRE_AMPERE is False
# and the port resolves bfloat16.
#
# bfloat16 is the right answer here for a reason that has NOTHING to do with
# speed, and this is the one place this rig's reasoning inverts a sibling's:
#
#   * XLA:CPU has no bf16 datapath. It upconverts to fp32 in front of every use —
#     precisely the emulation that costs the Turing siblings 54% of decode time,
#     and the reason they resolve float16 instead.
#   * float16 would not help. Turing has fp16 tensor cores to escape into; a CPU
#     has no 16-bit float datapath of any kind, so fp16 is upconverted exactly
#     the same way. The escape the GPU rigs have does not exist here.
#   * float32 storage WOULD avoid the conversion and does not fit: 18.5 GB of
#     weights against 14.3 GiB of RAM. MEASURED HERE as arithmetic, not attempted.
#
# So the dtype tax is unavoidable rather than a bug, and what makes it
# unavoidable is memory. Do not open a ticket to remove it; the fix that worked
# nowhere on Turing (three placements tried and measured) has one fewer option
# here, not one more.
DTYPE=bfloat16

# auto follows the compute dtype, which is what it should do. fp8 is refused
# outright by resolve_cache_dtype() rather than silently downgraded, and there is
# no fp8 anything on a CPU.
KV_CACHE_DTYPE=auto

# --- Memory: the binding constraint ------------------------------------------
# On an accelerator the weights sit in a device budget you can read off a spec
# sheet and the allocator raises when you exceed it. Here they sit in the same
# RAM as everything else on the machine and exceeding it is ACCEPTED, then paid
# for in swap. A thrashing serve is indistinguishable from a loading one, which
# is why check_host_capacity does the arithmetic before a load rather than after.
#
# Weight footprints, INHERITED from gpu-jax-g5g-2b's 2026-08-26 measurement. They
# are properties of the checkpoint and the levers, not of the device, so they
# carry — and every one of that run's predictions landed within 1%:
#
#   ple0 (dense)                9.257 GB
#   ple8                        6.927 GB   (-2.330)
#   ple4                        5.752 GB   (-3.505)
#   ple0 + int8_lm_head         9.660 GB   (+0.403)
#   ple4 + int8_lm_head         6.155 GB   (-3.102)

# PLE_BITS=4 carries over from the GPU siblings unconditionally: a pure memory
# win, and decode is IDENTICAL across ple0/ple8/ple4 there because the per-layer
# embedding table is a gather, never a matmul, so decode never streams it.
PLE_BITS=4

# INT8_LM_HEAD DOES NOT carry over, and this default is deliberately INVERTED
# from every GPU sibling. It ADDS 0.403 GB — an int8 copy of the tied embedding
# placed alongside the original — to buy +2.3% throughput by halving the bytes
# READ per decode step. That is a memory-bandwidth trade. It is the wrong trade
# on a host where the constraint is resident bytes and the surplus goes to swap:
# you would pay 0.4 GB of paging to save bandwidth you are not short of.
#
# It is also not what its name suggests on any rig here — jax_e_model.py
# dequantizes the int8 table to the compute dtype IN FULL (0.75 GiB) on every
# decode step and then runs the same matmul. Turn it on only after
# check_host_capacity reports real headroom.
INT8_LM_HEAD=0

# --- Swap, and why it took two goes ------------------------------------------
# MEASURED HERE 2026-08-29. The host had NO swap and 3.3 GB of MemAvailable
# against 5.75 GB of weights, so a serve could not start at all.
#
# `fallocate` + `mkswap` + `swapon` is the remedy the parent rig applies in its
# cloud-init, and it FAILS on this host with a bare:
#
#     swapon: /swapfile: swapon failed: Invalid argument
#
# The actual reason appears only in dmesg — `BTRFS warning (device vdb):
# swapfile must not be copy-on-write` — and the root filesystem here is btrfs.
# The remedy is btrfs-progs' own helper, which sets NOCOW and disables
# compression on the file it creates:
#
#     sudo btrfs filesystem mkswapfile --size 16G /swapfile
#     sudo swapon /swapfile
#     echo '/swapfile none swap sw 0 0' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
#
# Worth keeping for the same reason the parent kept its `mkswap -q` incident: the
# error names the syscall and not the cause, so the failure reads as a broken
# script rather than a filesystem that will not host that file.

# --- Context -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Lowered from the GPU siblings' 4096. Their limit is where the prefill transient
# stopped fitting in 14.07 GB of device memory (MEASURED on G5g 2026-08-26: 4,105
# tokens ok, 5,120 infeasible). Here the limit is patience — prefill is linear in
# the PADDED bucket with no accelerator to hide it, and a bucket you will not wait
# for is not a bucket worth compiling.
#
# 2048 > sliding_window (512), so window_kv still auto-resolves True, which is
# what keeps the KV cache at 30 MiB and irrelevant. See the KV note below.
MAX_MODEL_LEN=2048

# Not a knob anywhere in this monorepo. Gemma4EModelJAX raises
# NotImplementedError for B > 1 and the decode step donates its KV buffers.
MAX_NUM_SEQS=1

# Bounds prefill temporaries, and is STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE while window_kv
# resolves True — jax_e_model.py raises `prefill_chunk_size requires
# window_kv=False`, and window_kv auto-resolves True whenever
# max_model_len > sliding_window, which 2048 > 512 is. Empty = one-shot prefill.
# Recorded because it looks like the obvious remedy for a slow prefill and is not
# reachable without also setting window_kv=off, which is untested here.
PREFILL_CHUNK_SIZE=

# --- KV cache is free here, and that is arithmetic not luck ------------------
# INHERITED reasoning, verified against MODELS.md. E2B has 35 layers but only 15
# own a KV cache (first_kv_shared_layer_idx=15); with window_kv on, the 12
# sliding ones are capped at the 512-slot ring and only 3 full-attention layers
# hold the full context. At 2048 tokens, float16, B=1, one KV head that is about
# 18 MiB in total.
#
# So NEVER size this rig's context from KV arithmetic. The thing that costs
# memory is the prefill transient, which is ~1.6 GB flat below a 4K bucket —
# roughly ninety times the entire KV cache.

# --- Runtime -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Force the CPU backend rather than hoping it is the only one. This machine also
# hosts the GPU sibling rigs, so a CUDA plugin present in site-packages would
# silently hand this rig a GPU and every number it produced would be mislabelled.
# verify_cpu_backend asserts the resolved device really is a CpuDevice.
JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu

# Bind LOOPBACK, unlike every cloud sibling, which binds 0.0.0.0 because reaching
# the box from elsewhere is the entire point there. This server has no
# authentication of any kind and this host is not behind a cloud security group.
JAX_HOST=127.0.0.1
JAX_PORT=8000

# There is no JAX_PIP_SPEC key here, unlike every cloud sibling. Those rigs
# render a bootstrap that pip-installs onto a machine they just created, so the
# spec has to be configuration. Nothing bootstraps here — `make install` reads
# requirements.txt, which is the single place the spec lives. What changed with
# it is worth recording: it is plain `jax`, not `jax[cuda13]`, so the install is
# seconds rather than ~3 GB of CUDA wheels this rig would never dispatch to.

# The XLA compilation cache. NOT ephemeral here, unlike every cloud sibling,
# because there is no instance to reclaim — so the S3 sync, the systemd timer and
# the operator-supplied bucket URI are all deleted rather than ported.
#
# The default is ALSO the path ports/gemma4/jax_e_model.py hardcodes at import,
# and that is deliberate. On the cloud rigs those two disagree and the port wins,
# because jax_openai_server.py sets the directory at line 72 and then imports the
# port at line 80: the configured directory stayed empty forever while
# ~/.cache/jax_compilation_cache filled up (MEASURED on G5g 2026-08-27, 447 files
# in the wrong place, 0 in the right one). Making the default agree is what makes
# this knob honest here. test_both_modules_resolve_the_cache_dir_the_same_way
# pins it.
JAX_COMPILATION_CACHE_DIR=~/.cache/jax_compilation_cache

# Caps the OpenMP/BLAS pools only. It does NOT resize XLA:CPU's own intra-op
# thread pool — JAX exposes no documented flag for that, and the honest lever is
# `taskset` or a cgroup on the whole process. Empty = let everything size itself
# from the host, which is what you want on a box that is not doing anything else.
XLA_CPU_THREADS=

# --- What this rig has NOT measured ------------------------------------------
# Everything. There is no throughput number here yet, and the ones in the
# inherited prose (12.4 / 12.8 / 13.10 tok/s) are all Turing GPU results from
# `gpu-jax-g5g-2b`. Do not difference a number from this rig against one of them
# and read it as a CPU-vs-GPU result until this rig has recorded its own run
# under benchmarks/runs/ with the host written into the report.
